--- !u!114 &1582174451
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1582174449}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "Protozoa are tiny, mostly single-celled organisms that are commonly found
    in water, moist soil, and other damp environments. Although they consist of only
    one cell, they can perform all the basic activities needed for life, such as
    moving, feeding, growing, and reproducing.\nAmoeba is a common example of a protozoan.
    It is a microscopic, single-celled organism usually found in freshwater environments
    such as ponds and lakes. Amoeba does not have a fixed body shape. Instead, its
    body constantly changes shape as it moves.\nAmoeba moves and captures food using
    temporary finger-like extensions called pseudopodia, which means \u201Cfalse
    feet.\u201D It extends its pseudopodia around tiny food particles and surrounds
    them. The food is then enclosed inside a food vacuole, where it is digested.\nThe
    cell of an amoeba contains cytoplasm, a cell membrane, and a nucleus. The cell
    membrane forms the outer boundary and controls the movement of substances into
    and out of the cell. The nucleus controls the activities of the cell. Amoeba
    also has a contractile vacuole, which helps remove excess water from the cell
    and maintain water balance.\nAmoeba can respond to changes in its surroundings
    and move toward food or away from unfavorable conditions. Despite being made
    of just one cell, it can carry out many important life processes independently.
    This makes amoeba a simple and useful example for understanding how single-celled
    organisms survive and function."
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: ecf77945acdc26144b2f70e021c9fd31, type: 2}
